Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Custom "Cancel" Back Button (in your XAML page)

```xml
<!-- EditProductPage.xaml -->
<ContentPage ...>
    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                           Command="{Binding BackCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 2. Navigation Guard with Confirmation Dialog (in AppShell.xaml.cs)

```csharp
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    if (Shell.Current.CurrentPage is EditProductPage page
        && page.HasUnsavedChanges
        && args.Source == ShellNavigationSource.Pop)
    {
        var deferral = args.GetDeferral();

        bool discard = await Shell.Current.CurrentPage.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard and leave?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();

        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

## 3. ViewModel BackCommand

```csharp
[RelayCommand]
private async Task Back()
{
    // This triggers OnNavigating, so the guard above will fire
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

**Key points:**

- `GetDeferral()` is required for async checks — without it the navigation completes before your dialog returns.
- `args.Cancel()` stops the navigation if the user chooses "Stay".
- `TextOverride="Cancel"` replaces the back arrow with a "Cancel" label.
- The `Command` on `BackButtonBehavior` intercepts the button tap, letting you route it through `GoToAsync("..")` so the guard still fires.
